    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Decentralization is a prominent feature of education policy debates around the world. Proponents\r\nof decentralization argue that bringing decisions closer to the people improves school efficiency\r\nand quality by ensuring that schools are more responsive to local educational needs and\r\nempowering communities to hold schools accountable for education provision. In practice, the\r\neffects of decentralization vary substantially given that the implementation of these reforms\r\nrelies on local resources and management capacity. In this paper, I use a difference-indifferences\r\nmodel to estimate the effects of decentralization on achievement and teacher effort in\r\nIndonesia by leveraging school level longitudinal data from the Indonesian Family Life Survey. I\r\nfind no overall effect of decentralization on achievement, but a negative effect on teacher effort,\r\nparticularly in rural areas and among schools with inactive school committees. These findings\r\ndemonstrate the limits of decentralization in resource-constrained settings.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
